    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing stolen base upside and favorable platoon splits against mid-tier arms. **Corbin Carroll** (76.19) and **James Wood** (73.06) headline our lineup as the premier plays; **James Wood** specifically leverages a fantastic 0.429 xwOBA and the leadoff spot to anchor the offense, while **Corbin Carroll** continues to offer high-floor production despite a slight penalty for facing an elite hold pitcher. **Josh Naylor** (72.35) remains an essential start, bolstered by a +19% boost from the matchup against a struggling Tyler Phillips. We are leaning heavily into speed-positive environments today, evidenced by the high scores for **Mickey Moniak** (63.93) and **Jose Caballero** (60.94), who both benefit significantly from opposing catchers and pitchers with slow deliveries that maximize their stolen base potential.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Max Muncy**, who finished just outside the cut with a 51.27 score. While **Max Muncy** enjoys a park factor boost, he was ultimately edged out by the superior platoon alignment and matchup leverage held by **Zach McKinstry** and **Mickey Moniak**. The optimizer prioritized the latter\u2019s dynamic platoon advantages, leaving **Max Muncy** and **Cole Young** on the pine. We must remain vigilant regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**; while their projections hold at 59.82 and 55.54 respectively, any late shift in batting order\u2014specifically for **Alex Freeland**, whose value is sensitive to the #8 spot\u2014could necessitate a real-time adjustment to prevent efficiency leaks. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** presents a notable risk; his score of 51.87 is suppressed by facing a sub-3.00 xERA pitcher in Dean Kremer, despite the positive park factors, making him the most fragile link in today's starting unit.",
